<!--ID=5B95B1CC-2C7B-494F-B746-CF22A0E779B7;Version=1|{"Locales":{"en-US":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]},"en-GB":{"AutoApplyCustomLexiconFiles":[{}]}}}-->
<speak xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis"
       xmlns:mstts="http://www.w3.org/2001/mstts"
       xmlns:emo="http://www.w3.org/2009/10/emotionml"
       version="1.0"
       xml:lang="en-GB">
  <voice name="en-GB-OllieMultilingualNeural">
    <prosody rate="-11.25%" pitch="-2.80%">
            Mary gives birth to her firstborn son, wraps Him in swaddling cloths, and lays Him in a manger, because there was no place for them in the guest room.<break time="500ms"/>
      Luke's wording is simple and careful, and the simplicity itself becomes part of the wonder.<break time="500ms"/>
      The promised Son of the Most High enters the world in humility, wrapped by His mother and laid where animals were fed.<break time="1000ms"/>
      The throne promised by Gabriel is not denied by the manger; it is simply hidden beneath humility at the beginning.<break time="500ms"/>
      No human arrangement makes the child less who He is, and no lowly setting reduces the glory of what God has done.<break time="500ms"/>
      The nativity asks the listener to slow down enough to see holiness in smallness, majesty in meekness, and promise in an infant lying still.<break time="1800ms"/>
      The turning point comes by God's faithfulness.<break time="1000ms"/>
      Jesus is born in humility, yet His identity remains unchanged.<break time="1000ms"/>
      God comes near without display and without losing glory.<break time="1000ms"/>
      You can rest knowing God's nearness does not require worldly grandeur.<break time="1800ms"/>
      You can rest.<break time="3800ms"/>
    </prosody>
  </voice>
</speak>